# ==========================================================================
#  O64  Structure: depth, breadth, tangledness, cohesion
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    The Trail's hierarchy is two deep, one wide on average, untangled and
#    cohesive, which the structural metrics confirm; and gist, measured the
#    same way, shows sixty-two roots because its structure lives in
#    definitions the metrics do not read.
#
#  WHY
#    Five metrics describe the shape of the class tree. DITOnto is the
#    longest path from the root to a leaf: the Trail's is 2 (Place, then
#    Settlement), which scores 5, and gist's is 3. NOCOnto is the mean
#    number of children per non-root class: 1 for both. NACOnto is the mean
#    number of ancestors per leaf: under half for both, because most
#    classes are roots. TMOnto is the mean number of parents per class that
#    has one: 1 for both, which is to say no multiple inheritance anywhere,
#    and o32 said why. LCOMOnto, the paper's cohesion measure, is the mean
#    path length from leaf to root: about 1.4 for both.
#
#    The number that stands out is gist's sixty-two root classes out of
#    ninety-six. gist is not flat; it is defined. gist:Contract is
#    Agreement and isUnderJurisdictionOf some GovernmentOrganization, by
#    owl:equivalentClass, and a reasoner places it under Agreement. The
#    metric reads rdfs:subClassOf and sees a root. Run the metrics on the
#    reasoned ontology and the tree appears; run them on the asserted one
#    and gist looks like a list. That is the first thing to know about
#    structural metrics: they measure what was written, and an ontology
#    that lets the reasoner do the tangling (o32, o55) will score as if it
#    had no structure at all.
#
#    The scores themselves are on the measured line for the Trail and in
#    the diagram for both. On structure, both ontologies score 5 on nearly
#    everything, which says less than it seems: a small, shallow, single-
#    parented hierarchy is the easy case, and OQuaRE's thresholds were set
#    for ontologies with thousands of classes.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       metric      what                                  Trail        gist        threshold for 5
#       DITOnto     longest root-to-leaf path             2  (5)       3  (4)      at most 2
#       NOCOnto     mean children per non-root class      1  (5)       1  (5)      at most 3
#       NACOnto     mean ancestors per leaf               0.43 (5)     0.46 (5)    at most 2
#       TMOnto      mean parents per class with a parent  1  (5)       1  (5)      at most 2
#       LCOMOnto    mean leaf-to-root path length         1.43 (5)     1.46 (5)    at most 2
#
#       gist: 62 roots of 96 classes, asserted. Reasoned, most of them have a parent.
#       the metrics read rdfs:subClassOf; a defined hierarchy is invisible to them
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - Depth, breadth, ancestors, tangledness, cohesion: five numbers,
#      and for a small ontology all five come out well.
#    - Structural metrics read what is asserted. An ontology that defines
#      its classes and lets the reasoner classify looks flat to them.
#    - Measure the reasoned ontology as well as the written one when the
#      structure is in the definitions.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  DATA      01-vocabulary.ttl
#  RUNS IN   the browser; the metrics come from scripts/metrics.py; the gist column from the same script on gistCore.ttl
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F10-quality%2Fo64-structure-depth-breadth-tangledness-cohesion.ttl
#  SOURCES   Duque-Ramos, Fernandez-Breis, Stevens and Aussenac-Gilles, OQuaRE: a SQuaRE-based approach for evaluating the quality of ontologies, JRPIT 43(2), 2011
#            http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/~stevensr/papers/OQuareProof.pdf
#            Guarino and Welty, An overview of OntoClean, Handbook on Ontologies, 2009
#            https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-92673-3_9
#  MEASURED  parses, 6 triples; OQuaRE DIT 2 (5), ANOnto 2.00 (5); 'Depth of every class in the ' 17 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

<https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema>
    a                   owl:Ontology ;
    skos:prefLabel      "The Bookshop Trail schema"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "Structural metrics: see the MEASURED line, computed over the shipped vocabulary. gist's, for comparison, are in this lesson's diagram."@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Depth of every class in the shipped vocabulary  (on the data)
#  PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
#  PREFIX owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>
#  SELECT ?class (COUNT(?up) AS ?depth) WHERE {
#    ?class a owl:Class . FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?class), "https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#"))
#    OPTIONAL { ?class rdfs:subClassOf+ ?up . FILTER(isIRI(?up)) }
#  } GROUP BY ?class ORDER BY DESC(?depth) ?class
